From 624c6d456102c2424da4e3a9deb5d2eae73bf15e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 19:19:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Restart T3-rtx on a new cumulative ack in the express SACK
 path

RFC 4960 section 6.3.2 rule R3 (RFC 9260 likewise): whenever a SACK
acknowledges the DATA chunk with the earliest outstanding TSN for an
address, restart that address's T3-rtx timer with its current RTO if data
is still outstanding.

sctp_handle_sack() does this: when the cumulative ack moved it stops the
send timers before re-arming them. sctp_express_handle_sack(), which
handles every SACK without gap blocks, only calls sctp_timer_start(), and
sctp_timer_start() leaves an already running timer alone. So as long as
data stays in flight, T3 expires one RTO after it was first armed, no
matter how promptly the peer acknowledges.

Each such expiry doubles the RTO and re-arms the timer with the doubled
value; the next SACK only restores the RTO for the next arming. Steady
state is one timeout every 2 x RTO (1.25/s with a 400 ms minimum RTO).
Each expiry also marks every chunk older than srtt + 4 * rttvar for
retransmission. A peer that delays its SACK for a lone packet, as dcSCTP
(libwebrtc) does for up to 200 ms, regularly has such chunks
outstanding, so these spurious timeouts cut cwnd to one MTU and halve
ssthresh down to 4 MTU, capping throughput at a few MTU per RTT.

It shows whenever the RTT exceeds the sender's write interval or the
sender is backlogged. With a 50 ms write interval and a peer that acks
like dcSCTP, measured over 25 s with no loss:

  RTT 155 ms, 293 KiB/s offered: 1.22 timeouts/s, 119 KiB/s delivered,
    13 s p95 latency; with this change 0 timeouts, 287 KiB/s, 85 ms
  RTT 318 ms, 59 KiB/s offered: 1.76 timeouts/s, 10 s p95 latency;
    with this change 0 timeouts, 159 ms

With 1-3 % random loss the change is never worse and usually better.

Stop the send timer of each destination with data in flight when the
cumulative ack advanced, as sctp_handle_sack() does, so the following
sctp_timer_start() re-arms it with the current RTO. dcSCTP
(RetransmissionQueue::HandleSack) and Pion restart T3 the same way.

Written with assistance from Claude Code.
---
 usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_indata.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_indata.c b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_indata.c
index 115ec1d..67598c5 100755
--- a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_indata.c
+++ b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp_indata.c
@@ -3916,6 +3916,7 @@ sctp_express_handle_sack(struct sctp_tcb *stcb, uint32_t cumack,
 	int win_probe_recovered = 0;
 	int j, done_once = 0;
 	int rto_ok = 1;
+	int accum_moved;
 	uint32_t send_s;
 
 	if (SCTP_BASE_SYSCTL(sctp_logging_level) & SCTP_LOG_SACK_ARRIVALS_ENABLE) {
@@ -3932,6 +3933,8 @@ sctp_express_handle_sack(struct sctp_tcb *stcb, uint32_t cumack,
 #endif
 	asoc = &stcb->asoc;
 	old_rwnd = asoc->peers_rwnd;
+	/* RFC 4960 6.3.2 R3: a new cumulative ack restarts T3, as the slow path does */
+	accum_moved = SCTP_TSN_GT(cumack, asoc->last_acked_seq);
 	if (SCTP_TSN_GT(asoc->last_acked_seq, cumack)) {
 		/* old ack */
 		return;
@@ -4264,6 +4267,10 @@ again:
 		}
 		if (net->flight_size) {
 			j++;
+			if (accum_moved && (done_once == 0)) {
+				sctp_timer_stop(SCTP_TIMER_TYPE_SEND, stcb->sctp_ep,
+				                stcb, net, SCTP_FROM_SCTP_INDATA + SCTP_LOC_32);
+			}
 			sctp_timer_start(SCTP_TIMER_TYPE_SEND, stcb->sctp_ep, stcb, net);
 			if (net->window_probe) {
 				net->window_probe = 0;
-- 
